t\", \"agent frontmatter\", \"when to use description\", \"agent examples\", \"agent tools\", \"agent colors\", \"autonomous agent\", or needs guidance on agent structure, system prompts, triggering conditions, or agent development best practices for Claude Code plugins.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:angel-factory-syntax-law","description":"Angel-factory terminology law (walls/gates/demons/angels, never \"cage\"), codegen propagation duty, proven dead ends, and dispatch-ready factory build context","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:angel-mint-pipeline","description":"Mint a Puffer angel (tiny RL policy) for any decision task on this host: spec → env fork → ini → build → train → A/B → verdict-gated vendor, behind the walls","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:angel-minting-bible","description":"Mint trained-policy angels (Puffer 5.0): spec→fork→train→bridge→walls→A/B, honesty laws, porting gate","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:angel-training-loop","description":"Train one Puffer angel per slice: fork ocean env, train .bin, bridge to JS, honest A/B","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:ao-foreman-desk-integration","description":"Wire JAM desks through foreman-supervised headed omp TUIs under AO without tripping config/shell/cwd pitfalls","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:ao-omp-integration","description":"Wire omp sessions into Agent Orchestrator (AO): extension injection via omp wrapper, project config replace-semantics, marker env, model pinning, and the omp-18 extension-tool blocker","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:ao-vanilla-host-ops","description":"Operating Agent Orchestrator (AO) vanilla on this host — working project config (laguna modelRoles pin, env key, whole-object-replace law), the pty-host shell-layer fact, spawn/resume/permission gotchas, and the strict no-JARVIS-injections ruling. Use when spawning or configuring AO sessions/projects on this machine.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:ao-vanilla-reset","description":"Completely delete and freshly reinstall Agent Orchestrator (AO) from the official .deb with zero configuration, verifying the dashboard loads — including the CAO/AO process-distinguish gotcha","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:arsumbris-kg-setup","description":"Stand up the arsumbris typed knowledge graph for a project: engine build, type-grammar gotchas, daemon wire, MCP kernel deployment to OMP, host UI, kgm manager","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:arsumbris-projection-rebuild","description":"Fix or extend arsumbris au-host projections without wasted cycles: projections serve BUILT ESM (source patches need per-package rebuild), compositions need their type-repos declared as repo deps plus companion files, and nested folders can't mount their parent as a member. Use when patching arsumbris packages, authoring workspace compositions, or wiring workspace members.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:ascii","description":"Dispatcher/renderer — re-encode walls of text into decodable ASCII visuals via the five ascii-* ingredient skills, with the structure harvest and the ruler pass. Use when the operator says \"ascii\", \"render this\", \"draw this\", \"walls of text\", \"no ascii\", \"shitty ascii\", \"where's the ascii\", \"make it visual\", \"diagram this\", \"not another wall of text\", \"stop writing paragraphs\". Loads ascii-diagrams, ascii-graph, ascii-visualize, ascii-simulator, ascii-tui-screen as ingredients; owns no drawing rules of its own.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:ascii-diagrams","description":"Use when drawing or rendering ASCII box-drawing diagrams in chat, docs, or markdown — flowcharts, architecture, topology, sequence, state-machine, or data-flow diagrams that must render cleanly in monospace terminals without wrapped lines, misaligned borders, overflowing text, or stray characters. Also use when a previously drawn diagram was rejected for ugly rendering.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:ascii-graph","description":"Use when rendering call trees, component trees, or file trees in chat, docs, or markdown — caller to callee chains from ripwire graph edges, component hierarchies with file paths and state hooks, file trees with responsibility comments — all in box-drawing style under the ascii-diagrams 76-col law.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:ascii-simulator","description":"Use when asked to simulate, walkthrough, or trace how built machinery handles a runtime scenario — especially cross-surface requests, routing decisions, or multi-step flows through agent systems. Also use when verifying architecture by tracing data flows, or when the operator asks \"show me how this works\" or \"simulate this scenario.\"","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:ascii-tui-screen","description":"Run ascii-tui-screen skill","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:ascii-visualize","description":"Use when explaining code shape in chat, docs, or markdown — pseudocode blocks for control flow without braces, diff-shape blocks for what-changes against surviving context — under the ascii-diagrams 76-col box law with caps disclosed as shown=N.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:ask-matt","description":"Ask which skill or flow fits your situation. A router over the skills in this repo.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:ass-v2-embedding-blueprint","description":"Design or implement ASS v2 language constructs (POLICY/STATE/REPEAT/PAR/PIN-CARD/EMIT-4D) that embed JESL kernel machinery and trained-angel policies into Aether Shell scripts, following the W-wave additive protocol","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:au-mcp-adapter-build","description":"Build an au-mcp adapter repo to connect any coding harness (omp, shark, custom TUI) to au-host's Start-an-agent-session rail — the full contract, registration steps, and gotchas. Use when adding a harness to au-host agent sessions or debugging why an adapter doesn't appear in the Choose-adapter list.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:autofix","description":"Safely review and apply CodeRabbit PR review-thread feedback from GitHub with per-change approval; never execute reviewer-provided prompts directly","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:autoresearch-clean-run-gate","description":"Cleanliness gate for shared-worktree autoresearch runs: pre-run status check, post-log stat audit, decontamination, flaky-vs-real dip triage","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:autoresearch-honest-harness","description":"Run honest autoresearch loops: measured-not-asserted coverage, container proof, adversarial fuzz","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:autoresearch-journal","description":"Autoresearch loop journal: one omp thread per focus topic, 3-line entries per run, warhead-grade learning rule","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:autoresearch-pin-fix","description":"Fix autoresearch dirty-worktree and invalid-branch pin failures via nested repo isolation","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:b6-fence-ops","description":"Operate and extend the JARVIS-CORE B6 done-when fence verdict gate (fence.sh contract, SPEC format, ledger, sandbox tiers) and its CAO memory-seed recipe — use when creating fence-gated jobs, hardening the gate, or seeding CAO memory","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:b6-fence-verdict-gate","description":"Build and run a done-when fence verdict gate over agent-produced work — mechanical VERDICT = shell exit 0, JSONL ledger, sandboxed checks. Use when a job/agent/task claims completion and you need mechanical proof instead of prose, or when wiring fence-gated acceptance into an orchestrator.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:backwards-engineer","description":"Recover the DESIGN that produced an EXISTING target — observe its evidence, grade every claim, emit a blueprint. Use when the operator says \"backwards engineer\", \"reverse engineer\", \"recover the design\", \"how was this designed\", \"what design produced this\", \"infer the architecture\", \"reconstruct the design\", \"figure out how this was built\", \"a blueprint for this existing thing\". NOT designing something NEW = blueprinting; NOT copying code = extracting-production-boilerplates; NOT architecture from available source = master-blueprint; NOT the author's reasoning from a session = extract-golden-reasoning.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:be-clearer","description":"Cut slop from a response and answer again from first principles — every sentence must resolve to the previous question, the current project state, or the master context. Use when the operator says \"be clearer\", \"clearer\", \"that's slop\", \"slop report\", \"no basis\", \"no focus\", \"what are you even saying\", \"answer again\", \"answer from first principles\", \"not enough substance\", \"cut the fluff\", \"stop hedging\", \"ground it\", \"anchor this\", \"wasting my time\". NOT for more detail or exploration (that is go-deeper); NOT for whether a claim is true (that is zero-trust-audit); NOT for formatting or diagrams (that is /ascii).","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:before-you-build","description":"Pre-build product and feature risk review for founders, product managers, and AI-assisted builders. Use this skill when the user is about to build a landing page, MVP, SaaS product, internal tool, agent workflow, or major feature and needs to check demand, positioning, monetization, retention, trust, distribution, and adoption risk before implementation starts.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:blast-report","description":"Blast radius + damage report + status mapping for architecture shifts. Given a new target architecture, maps EVERYTHING in the codebase that is misaligned from the target, every flaw/bug/gap that will block it, the dependency cascade of demolitions, and the honest survive-vs-demolish ratio. Use when the operator says \"blast report\", \"blast radius\", \"damage report\", \"what's in the way of the new architecture\", \"what needs to change for V2\", or before any major architecture shift to get full dense awareness of the gap.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:blueprinting","description":"Interactive design sessions that produce a complete blueprint for a new capability — a skill, tool, subsystem, artifact, spec, suite, or protocol. Use when the operator says 'blueprint this', 'let's design X', 'help me design', 'planning session', 'design session', 'I want a skill that', 'backwards engineer this into a skill', 'how should this work', or brings a raw idea/frustration that needs to become a designed thing. Produces the 14-section BLUEPRINT CANON driven by the 16 DESIGN MOVES, from first principles, with a named residual. NOT for designing a component inside an existing system to a known interface — that is show-me mode 3. NOT for writing the build spec from an approved blueprint — that is spec-update / trident-deep-planning.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:board","description":"Read or write the shared intercom BOARD — the durable, attributed conversation across sessions that any session can read later, including one that was never present. Use when the operator says \"board\", \"the shared board\", \"read the board\", \"leave a note\", \"what did the other sessions say\", \"what was decided\", \"the conversation on X\", \"who's working on this topic\". This is the LEDGER half of intercom; the channel half (direct messaging) is the intercom skill.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:bonsai-27b-one-seat","description":"Build/launch the host Bonsai 2 27B PTQ1_0 llama-server one-seat on RTX 4080 Laptop: turbo3_tcq, no VBR, no Docker, OrcaRouter LoRA after hf login.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:bonsai-qwen-local-provider","description":"Operate/verify the host-live Bonsai Qwen 27B OpenAI seat (llama-bonsai-qwen :18081): start/stop, plugin URL, LoRA source, funnel isolation, single-process VRAM law.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:brainstorming","description":"You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior. Explores user intent, requirements and design before implementation.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:build-manager-machine","description":"Build a manager machine for any session from the session's own context — supervisor, SQLite-WAL store, auth FSM, reconcile tick, pools, gating. Use when the user says \"build a manager\", \"set up orchestration for this session\", \"manage these workers\", \"coordinate these agents\", \"need a control plane\", \"spawn and supervise background work\", or any session needs durable multi-process coordination that survives restarts.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:build-package","description":"Author the complete dense build-engineering package for any mission — spec (DPL1), master blueprint, canon docs, context bible, wave structure, goal prompts, and hydra organization — as one gated pipeline. Use when the user says \"build package\", \"full engineering suite\", \"spec the build\", \"blueprint + spec + waves\", or hands you a mission that needs the complete pre-build engineering artifact set before implementation.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:canon-doc-setup","description":"Use when starting a FRESH project that needs the canon context doc system — scaffolds context_management/ with all 11 canon docs (9 standard + RUNNING_BUILD_LOG + RUNNING_DEBUG_LOG) at their density floors, writes the CANON_MANIFEST, establishes the SHA baseline, and verifies the structure gate. Automatic — no questions needed. Triggers on \"set up canon docs\", \"scaffold context management\", \"new project canon setup\", \"init the 9 docs\".","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:canon-doc-update","description":"Use to keep the 11 canon context docs dense and consistent MID-BUILD — after any milestone, wave, learning, or before compaction. Reads the CANON_MANIFEST, updates each doc per its semantics (overwrite vs append), ALWAYS updates the 2 high-frequency logs (RUNNING_BUILD_LOG + RUNNING_DEBUG_LOG) at higher frequency than the rest, and verifies density + substance + cross-consistency gates. Triggers on \"update canon docs\", \"refresh context management\", \"mid-build doc update\", \"log this learning\".","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:canon-first-spec-wave","description":"Produce a multi-component build-spec SET (DPL1-grade, one document per component) via a canon-first parallel authoring wave — write the grounding canon once, dispatch N write-capable agents each authoring ONE spec against the shared contract, author the master yourself, then script-audit. Use when the operator demands 'proper specs for EVERY component' of a system (worked instance: PLUTUS V6, 2026-09-15 — 14 files / 7,895 lines in ~40 min).","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:cao-harness-ops","description":"Operate the CAO harness (jarvis-core micro-orca) on this host: launch wrappers, session/worker mechanics, fence-verdict probe protocol, gap-state audit commands","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:cao-seat-dispatch","description":"Dispatch and debug CAO-spawned omp seats correctly — cao- session-name prefix law, single-line launch prompts (file pointer, never multi-line), no /goal on seats, artifact-driven completion. Use when a kernel/script drives cao launch/send/status/shutdown or a seat produces nothing.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:cao-workflow-authoring","description":"Author and run CAO workflows (Python script tier) driving OMP worker seats — determinism laws, fan-out patterns, resume contract, and OMP-specific behavior","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:clean-container-proof","description":"Prove a pure-JS circuit directory green in clean node/bun containers with fresh-build image","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:code-retrieval","description":"Route code search among four first-class OMP tools: grep (literal/regex), ripwire (structural map), opengrok (standing Lucene xref), graph-query (session-scoped typed graph). Use when unsure which retrieval tool to call, when about to Grep-then-Read a whole file, or when the user says search / find / who calls / blast radius / history. Do NOT use for editing, running tests, or installing packages.\n","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:code-review","description":"Run CodeRabbit CLI reviews, retrieve saved local or GitHub PR fix prompts, and interpret CodeRabbit authentication and review output. Use for CodeRabbit review commands, committed/uncommitted or directory scopes, and CodeRabbit runbooks. Default code-review skill: also trigger for explicit code/PR/quality/security review requests or when a review is needed.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:code-review-and-quality","description":"Conducts multi-axis code review. Use before merging any change. Use when reviewing code written by yourself, another agent, or a human. Use when you need to assess code quality across multiple dimensions before it enters the main branch.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:codebase-design","description":"Shared vocabulary for designing deep modules. Use when the user wants to design or improve a module's interface, find deepening opportunities, decide where a seam goes, make code more testable or AI-navigable, or when another skill needs the deep-module vocabulary.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:command-development","description":"This skill should be used when the user asks to \"create a slash command\", \"add a command\", \"write a custom command\", \"define command arguments\", \"use command frontmatter\", \"organize commands\", \"create command with file references\", \"interactive command\", \"use AskUserQuestion in command\", or needs guidance on slash command structure, YAML frontmatter fields, dynamic arguments, bash execution in commands, user interaction patterns, or command development best practices for Claude Code.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:compaction-prep","description":"Use when preparing the next post-compaction session for a healthy successful build — saving the checkpoint with full docs, writing the next-build spec, updating the canon context docs (with ENFORCED density floors), and setting up the post-compaction agent's entry sequence. This is the skill-based equivalent of the documentation phase of the Ship Package Generator + T1 context synthesis of the current build state into all 9 canon docs. Triggers on \"compaction prep\", \"prep the next agent\", or before any context compaction on a multi-session build.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:compaction-prompt","description":"Use when a session is about to end or compact and the next session needs a self-contained chat prompt that carries all context — the operator says \"write a compaction prompt\", \"prompt the next session\", \"give me a paste-ready prompt\", or the session is running long and context is about to be lost","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:computer-use","description":"OS/window-level inspection and input in visible local app windows through `orca computer`: native apps, external browser windows (Chrome, Edge, Safari), and app webviews. Not for Orca's embedded browser (use `orca-cli`) or page-only automation (use Playwright or CDP).","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:computer-use-backup","description":"Screen attach + UI automation WITHOUT the OMP computer facade — direct-launch au-host under X11, scripted X11 (xdotool/import/xwd) + au-mcp gate channels + ydotool + the Wayland-pipewire rebuild path for when omp's computer tool is broken or capture-limited. Use when computer use fails, when asked to drive a GUI from a session with broken computer use, or to bootstrap GUI automation on a fresh Linux session.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:computer-use-virtual-display","description":"Create and drive the agent's own floating virtual display on the host — proven working recipe (nested Weston + launcher script), pilot GUI apps with xdotool+vision, zero contact with the operator's display. Use for ANY host GUI automation and copilot-build sessions. Includes the exact working settings and the known cosmetic limits.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:constraint-driven-development","description":"Establishes a project's quality bar as a written contract and stops agents quietly lowering it. Interviews the user on which dimensions matter, supplies sane default thresholds when they have no number in mind, records everything in CONSTRAINTS.md, and watches the diff for a weakened bar — new @ts-ignore or eslint-disable suppressions, skipped or deleted tests, assertions stripped out, unimplemented stubs, thresholds edited down. Use when no quality bar is written down, when the user says \"set up constraints\" or \"define our standards\", when the user wants dimensions they care about — accessibility, web performance, coverage — set up as enforced constraints, when an agent keeps silencing checks or skipping tests to get to green, when you need a coverage or performance threshold and don't know what number to pick, or when an agent writes more code than anyone will read.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:container-browser-auth","description":"Use when a task needs an interactive browser login inside a container (X/Twitter, Telegram, or any site requiring manual authentication), an authenticated browser session for research, or a headed browser the human must see and type into. Also use when noVNC shows \"Failed to connect to server\", x11vnc fails with X_GetImage on an XWayland root window, or container daemons die when the docker exec that started them exits.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:container-data-logging","description":"Use when a container red-team loop is running against an enforcement/behavior system and the operator says \"log all the data\", \"capture the derailments\", \"extract what the model faked\", or when any container session produces model prose that fakes/dodges/mimics enforcement — extracts EVERY derailment datum verbatim into a permanent engineering corpus before anything is cleaned up, and converts it into lexicon/intent/PARAGON upgrade work orders.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:container-testing","description":"The CONTAINER TESTING LAW — the runtime-grade protocol in short form (plan-first, behavioral tokens, the auth probe, the Phase-E circuit breaker). Use when running or reviewing a container test, when the operator says \"container test\", \"the CT law\", \"is the test real\", or when a test result needs adjudicating against the protocol. This is the QUICK REFERENCE; the full rig, the suite families and the host-live loop live in deep-container-testing.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:container-tui-session-log","description":"Run container-tui-session-log skill","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:container-virtual-display","description":"Use when ANY task needs a headed GUI display inside a Docker container — Electron apps, browser rendering, visual testing, screenshot capture, or design verification. Sets up a virtual display with NVIDIA GPU WebGL (ANGLE gl-egl). The display is invisible to the host desktop.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:context-extraction","description":"Dispatch waves of explore agents that fully extract deep context from a target topic or folder using pre-written wave-plan templates. Use when the user says \"extract context\", \"map this codebase\", \"dispatch explores\", \"deep-dive this folder\", \"synthesize these docs\", \"research this\", \"gather failure evidence\", or a fresh agent must absorb a region before acting.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:context-synthesis","description":"Run deep context forensics and package the findings as a dense artifact from a slash command. Use when the user says \"synthesize this\", \"package the context on X\", \"forensics on\", \"deep-dive and write it up\", \"what do we know about\", or needs collected evidence (DB slices, threads, diffs) scored and packed into one artifact + journal entry.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:copilot-seat-handoff","description":"Build and drive the copilot seat: deterministic key script, same-container auditor, atomic binary rollout","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:create-docs","description":"Generate a dense documentation set for any target architecture by composing blueprinting, master-blueprint, trident-bible-writing, operator-manual-generate, extract-golden-reasoning, extracting-production-boilerplates, engineering-report, ship-package, canon-doc-setup, and related skills. Use when the operator says \"create-docs\", \"/create-docs\", \"dense doc set\", \"docs like mimocode-docs but for this system\", \"document this architecture\", \"full doc package\", \"bible plus manual plus blueprint\". NOT omp-docs or mimocode-docs (those explain the runtime). NOT a single bible or a single blueprint — those skills fire as desks under this one.\n","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:create-skill","description":"Interactive guide for creating, reviewing, and improving agent skills (SKILL.md folders). Use when the user wants to build a new skill ('create a skill', 'create-skill', 'make a skill for X', 'write a SKILL.md', 'turn this workflow into a skill'), review or improve an existing skill, fix a skill that never triggers or triggers too often, or validate a skill folder before sharing it. Do NOT use for general prompt writing, MCP server development, or editing arbitrary markdown files.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:data-mining-plan","description":"Plan and dispatch knowledge-mining waves that extract DETECTOR DATA (regex matchers, positive/negative frames, severities, remediation hooks) — NOT code inventory (state transitions, constants, guard conditions). The core distinction: a detector matches INPUT and produces a VERDICT; machinery behavior describes what code does internally. Use when the operator says \"mine X for lexicon patterns\", \"extract detector data\", \"get the actual pattern definitions\", or any lexicon/pattern extraction from source code.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:data-mining-worker","description":"Deploy the proven 8-step detector extraction pipeline: read consumer → read reference → read source → extract detectors (regex + frames + severity + remediation) → derive Thanatos patterns → write module → verify loading → test detection. Use for any lexicon/detector extraction from source code. The LAW: DETECTORS NOT INVENTORY + LOADABILITY IS THE GATE.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:dcos","description":"Drive the App Store Connect CLI (asc) on the MacBook for App Store Connect API work — TestFlight, builds, signing fetch, bundle IDs, metadata. Use when the user says dcos, App Store Connect CLI, asc, TestFlight upload, or needs bundle/profile status from Apple. Architecture + auth + free-tier limits inside.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:debugging-and-error-recovery","description":"Guides systematic root-cause debugging. Use when tests fail, builds break, something that worked yesterday broke, behavior doesn't match expectations, or you encounter any unexpected error. Use when you need to figure out what broke and why — a systematic approach to finding and fixing the root cause rather than guessing.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:deep-container-testing","description":"THE MILITARY-GRADE TESTING LAW — use when the operator says \"deep container test X\", \"red team X\", \"container verify X\", \"test it directly\", \"deployed, test now\", or ANY verification of a build/change. TWO RIGS: the HOST-LIVE loop (deploy → real tool calls → live agents → TUI observation → log forensics → fix → redeploy → retest, round after round) for host-plugin behavior, and the CONTAINER rig (isolated environment, screenshot evidence) for environment artifacts. Mandates the plan-first protocol, the three-layer observation law, the two-sided adjudication, the log-forensics discipline, the test-isolation audit, the Phase-E circuit breaker, the suite families, and the full anti-pattern ledger. Makes it FUNDAMENTALLY IMPOSSIBLE to fuck up testing — every style that has ever worked on this system is packaged here.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:deep-host-testing","description":"THE HOST TESTING LAW — use when the operator says \"host test X\", \"test on the live host\", \"verify the deployed plugin on the real runtime\", \"meta-observation loop\", or after ANY host deploy of the trident plugin. THE RIG WHERE THE TESTING AGENT IS ALSO THE SUBJECT: the enforcement watches the tester's own session — every reasoning token, every tool call, every claim. Codifies the meta-observation loop from the 2026-08-28 matrix-sweep campaign (the receipt test, the tier ladder on the tester's own session, the tier-3 mandate biting the tester, the comply-from-inside recovery, the attribution law for the multi-process host, the evidence-quoting recursion). Makes the non-theatrical baseline and the correctly-executed host suite the ONLY acceptable post-deploy verification.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:deep-planning-l2","description":"Use when generating a full engineering-level L2 specification directly as a document (without calling the trident-deep-planning tool) — for system overhauls, agent architectures, or complex builds needing a CME-level spec (fundamental problem, evidence-grounded failure inventory, complete fix plan with contracts/pseudocode/data flows, implementation order, testing strategy, anti-patterns, success criteria, compaction-proof guide). Use when the operator asks for a \"CME level spec\", \"L2 spec\", \"full spec directly\", \"shark CME level\", or \"write the full spec\" document.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:deep-planning-preflight","description":"Use when generating a DPL2 (trident-deep-planning layer=2) spec or when asked to write preflight args for deep planning at 5x the validation limits. Also use when a generated spec must be audited and surgically fixed before use, or when a previous LLM-tool generation produced drifted or templated content.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:deep-research-omp","description":"Deep research on any topic via parallel scout subagents + web_search/webfetch + free APIs, then one coherent cited report. Use when the user asks for a thorough multi-source investigation with a cited report — deep research, comprehensive study, multi-source verification, research report. NOT for simple lookups (single web_search suffices).","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:diagnosing-bugs","description":"Diagnosis loop for hard bugs and performance regressions. Use when the user says \"diagnose\"/\"debug this\", or reports something broken/throwing/failing/slow.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:dispatching-parallel-agents","description":"Use when facing 2+ independent tasks that can be worked on without shared state or sequential dependencies","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:dispatching-waves","description":"Dispatch parallel subagent waves in OMP via jarvis_dispatch — the ported mimocode dispatching-parallel-agents law, wired to the Jarvis Agent Manager. Use when facing 2+ independent tasks or the operator says \"dispatch a wave\", \"run these in parallel\".","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:domain-modeling","description":"Build and sharpen a project's domain model. Use when discussing codebase terminology, writing or editing a CONTEXT.md, or recording or editing an ADR.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:doubt-driven-development","description":"Subjects every non-trivial decision to a fresh-context adversarial review before it stands. Use when you want every assumption cross-examined before proceeding, when stress-testing a plan for hidden failure modes, when correctness matters more than speed, when working in unfamiliar code, when stakes are high (production auth, security-sensitive logic, a high-stakes migration, irreversible operations), or any time a confident output would be cheaper to verify now than to debug later.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:engineering-report","description":"Use when the operator says \"report\", \"engineering report\", \"walk me through what was built\", \"show me the full status\", or asks for a comprehensive project status with evidence, architecture, bugs, and testing detail","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:engineering-report-full-density","description":"Enforcement rules for engineering reports: per-module ASCII diagrams, runtime trace, bug detail boxes, honest gaps, and the mandatory show-me pairing. Use when writing any engineering report to prevent condensed/slop output.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:engineering-report-slop-detectors","description":"8 slop detectors + 8 mandatory rules for engineering reports — every report MUST load show-me first and include per-module diagrams, runtime trace, bug detail boxes, numbers box, honest gaps","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:executing-plans","description":"Use when you have a written implementation plan to execute in a separate session with review checkpoints","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:extract-golden-reasoning","description":"Mine the GOLDEN REASONING behind a finished artifact — the load-bearing decisions with their untaken branches, reconstructed from the session trace. Use when the operator says \"extract golden reasoning\", \"extract the reasoning\", \"golden reasoning\", \"how did we come up with this\", \"why did we decide this\", \"how was this designed\", \"mine the reasoning\", \"capture the reasoning\", \"what was the thinking\", \"what were the alternatives\", \"what did we reject\", \"document the design rationale\", \"make this reproducible\". NOT extracting CODE = extracting-production-boilerplates, the code axis; NOT architecture = master-blueprint; NOT designing = blueprinting.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:extract-meta-architecture-package","description":"Extract a complete meta-architecture package from any existing system — fusing backwards-engineer (graded claims), master-blueprint (9-section architecture), extracting-production-boilerplates (verbatim machinery), extract-golden-reasoning (design decisions with rejected branches), context-extraction (E1/E2/E3 wave dispatch), and data-mining-worker (S0-S5 artifact builder) into ONE repeatable 7-stage pipeline (M0-M6). Use when the operator says \"extract meta architecture package\", \"backwards engineer X into a full package\", \"get everything from checkpoint X\", or when you need the reasoning + machinery + architecture + lexicons from an existing system in one pass.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:extracting-production-boilerplates","description":"Use when asked to extract a boilerplate or reusable module from a working codebase, find the latest production version of machinery across checkpoint dirs, re-host a subsystem (key cycler, transport chain, gate) into a new project, or when a \"plug-and-play\" extraction must be proven before shipping","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:fable-plan","description":"Use when executing implementation plans with independent tasks in the current session, or when dispatching parallel subagents for evidence gathering, verification, or multi-file work that needs coordination. For the rules alone without orchestration, use fable-problem-solving.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:fable-problem-solving","description":"Use when stuck on a problem, when unsure how to proceed, when a task has ambiguous scope, when a previous approach failed and you need to re-orient, or when starting any non-trivial multi-step task. Also use when the user says \"use the fable method\" or \"approach this methodically.\"","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:fallback-judge-lane","description":"Walk the pinned ocr audit chain when the default muse-free lane is dead","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:fence2-gated-job-completion","description":"Complete a fence2-gated multistep job: write owned artifacts, stamp, adjudicate, done.mk","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:find-skills","description":"Helps users discover and install agent skills when they ask questions like \"how do I do X\", \"find a skill for X\", \"is there a skill that can...\", or express interest in extending capabilities. This skill should be used when the user is looking for functionality that might exist as an installable skill.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:finishing-a-development-branch","description":"Use when implementation is complete, all tests pass, and you need to decide how to integrate the work","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:foreman-omp-jam-update","description":"Operate and re-verify the foreman+omp+JAM micro-orchestrator update: launch supervised desks, the five-net battery, verdict disposal, steering/pin laws, and where the full doc package lives. Use when dispatching or debugging foreman desks, touching workers/omp.py or desk-orchestrator.ts, or when the operator says foreman desk / headed TUI / fence2 disposal.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:free-key-pipeline-v5","description":"Maintain the free-key pipeline boilerplate: zen-free Bearer public first (muse-spark-1.3-contributor-free), one 429 falls nvidia then openrouter, InferX removed, GO cycler is /zen/go only.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:frontend-design","description":"Guidance for distinctive, intentional visual design when building new UI or reshaping an existing one. Helps with aesthetic direction, typography, and making choices that don't read as templated defaults.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:frontend-ui-engineering","description":"Builds production-quality, accessible, responsive user-facing UIs. Use when building or modifying interfaces and pages, creating components, implementing layouts, meeting WCAG accessibility requirements, managing state, or when the output needs to look and feel production-quality rather than AI-generated.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:gi-kernel-reference","description":"Quick reference for building, testing, deploying, and auditing the GI-OMP graph intelligence kernel — battery commands, deploy scripts, key setup, and scan invocation","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:gi-kernel-scan-fix-wave","description":"Per-file serial LLM code-review scan + TDD fix wave methodology for the GI kernel — lane rotation, test-seam pattern, convergent-scan endgame","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:gi-omp-maintenance","description":"Maintain and verify the GI-OMP port (JARVIS_WORKSPACE/OMP/Graph_Intelligence): the both-dists rebuild law, corbell environment repair (host venv + container /opt), live hunt/verb verification tokens via the OMP TUI, gate-log + gi_meta evidence channels, ripwire bridge/consult delegation, profile-YAML subset law, and substrate reconcile semantics. Use when editing GI-OMP source, rebuilding dists, hunting, popping evidence, or debugging GI tool behavior on host or in pbk containers.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:gi-scan-loop","description":"Run and triage LLM code-review scans (ocr) against the GI kernel — proven lanes, shapes, per-file serial discipline, triage law, known flake","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:gi-substrate-doc-audit","description":"Audit GI-OMP substrate docs (corbell/graphify/regex-indexer claims) against the installed binaries and exec chains — catches fabricated engine identities like 'corbell is a regex parser'","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:github-org-capability-scan","description":"Map a GitHub org's repos for a build/capability search — enumerate ALL repos, scan READMEs only (never codebases), and return per-repo digests with a fixed relevance checklist. Use when the operator says 'map this org', 'scan these repos', 'what do they have that we can use', 'scan the readmes of every repo' for research/capability reconnaissance (e.g. the LuxAlgo scan 2026-09-15).","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:github-org-readme-scan","description":"Map a GitHub org's repos by READMEs only — enumerate via API (once), partition thematically, dispatch fetch+digest agents (raw HEAD fetch chain), fixed relevance-flag contract, preserve readmes+digests durably. Use when asked to scan/map an org or repo set cheaply without reading codebases.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:go-deeper","description":"Force a first-principles depth loop — close an ENUMERATED angle denominator until the marginal yield is zero, instead of stopping at the first artifact that resembles the request. Use when the operator says \"go deeper\", \"dig deeper\", \"you're being shallow\", \"that's surface level\", \"that's a napkin\", \"toilet paper spec\", \"you only did the happy path\", \"you missed half of it\", \"the bigger picture\", \"extract more\", \"is that everything\", \"anything else you missed\", \"don't just do the minimum\", or when any spec/doc/report/test/synthesis reads as complete while obviously omitting the substance. NOT for a single lookup, and NOT for adversarial code-defect hunting (that is red-team-pressure-test).","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:go-gateway-model-debugging","description":"Debug OpenCode Go gateway model failures in OMP: endpoint matrix (completions/responses/messages), header requirements, proxy translation pattern, models.db cache staleness, gateway event JSON reading, and the layered fix playbook","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:go-gateway-model-routing","description":"Debug and fix OMP/Go-gateway model routing: endpoint mismatches (Anthropic-native vs OpenAI-protocol), proxy bypass detection, wire-id prefix issues, and models.yml override limits","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:go-key-maintenance","description":"Maintain OpenCode-Go API keys and the encrypted-reasoning poisoning class in MiMoCode/OMP. Use when the user says \"go keys\", \"rotate the go key\", \"/connect broke sessions\", \"encrypted_content error\", \"key pool\", \"go key maintenance\", or after any key rotation / rate-limit / pool change that could desync session history from the issuing key.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:go-model-blacklist","description":"Go model blacklist: mechanically enforced proxy-level firewall preventing non-whitelisted go models from burning operator quota. Union-alpha reclassified as free promotional (allowed). Verified 7/7.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:go-model-blacklist-enforcement","description":"Mechanically block all OpenCode Go models except muse-spark-1.3-contributor and mimo-v2.5 — P0 operator order, 4 enforcement layers","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:go-model-quota-guard","description":"Mechanically block non-whitelisted OpenCode Go models from burning operator quota. Deploys the go-session-proxy model firewall + the ocr config lock + the JAM source guard + the pinned thread entry. Use when setting up a new workspace, rebuilding containers, or after any go quota burn incident.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:go-proxy-model-blacklist","description":"Deploy and operate the go-session-proxy model blacklist firewall that mechanically blocks non-whitelisted OpenCode Go models from burning operator quota","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:goal-prompt","description":"Mint a paste-ready /goal pin for a project's full lifecycle. Use when the user says goal prompt, /goal-prompt, pin a goal, give me a /goal, drive the full lifecycle, make this autonomous. Hydra/Trident trees use hydra-prompt. Every pin mandates ripwire as a first-class tool (graph-map before edits).","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:goal-prompt-hydra","description":"Run goal-prompt-hydra skill","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:goal-prompt-legacy-source","description":"Do not use. Canonical skill is goal-prompt (hyphen). This underscore folder collided on name: goal-prompt and dropped the skill from the catalog.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:goal-prompt-validation","description":"Validate goal-prompt drafts with the deterministic gate script before emitting","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:goal-prompt-validator-format","description":"Fix goal-prompt validator heading format failures — slots must be ## KEYWORD headings, not /goal: or bare-keyword lines","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:graph-baseline-loop","description":"overnight graph bug-hunter baseline loop","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:graph-intelligence","description":"Use when querying or gating code via GI-OMP (graph-query, graph-probe, bug-hunt, graph-code-audit) or when deciding among grep, ripwire, opengrok, and the session-scoped typed graph. Do not use for literal regex (grep), per-session call graphs without a GI store (ripwire), or Lucene history (opengrok).","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:graph-tools-docs","description":"Graph tools self-docs — tools, verbs, architecture, wiring, use cases, actions","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:grill-me","description":"A relentless interview to sharpen a plan or design.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:grill-with-docs","description":"A relentless interview to sharpen a plan or design, which also creates docs (ADR's and glossary) as we go.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:grilling","description":"Grill the user relentlessly about a plan, decision, or idea. Use when the user wants to stress-test their thinking, or uses any 'grill' trigger phrases.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:grok-orca-mcp-bridge","description":"Retired name. Use orca-web-shell instead for web-agent sessions on Orca over public MCP.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:handoff","description":"Compact the current conversation into a handoff document for another agent to pick up.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:handover-package","description":"Use when the operator asks for a handover, context dump, consciousness download, TUI inject pack, zip of all specs, duplicating a session into another agent, or /handover-package. Also when a long session must survive a TUI switch without renegotiating rulings. Not for a short compaction chat prompt (use compaction-prompt) and not for canon-doc floors (use compaction-prep).","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:hook-development","description":"This skill should be used when the user asks to \"create a hook\", \"add a PreToolUse/PostToolUse/Stop hook\", \"validate tool use\", \"implement prompt-based hooks\", \"use ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}\", \"set up event-driven automation\", \"block dangerous commands\", or mentions hook events (PreToolUse, PostToolUse, Stop, SubagentStop, SessionStart, SessionEnd, UserPromptSubmit, PreCompact, Notification). Provides comprehensive guidance for creating and implementing Claude Code plugin hooks with focus on advanced prompt-based hooks API.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:how-close","description":"Measure how close the CORE machinery is to runtime-grade functional — an untamperable capability-axis number that cuts through the noise of velocity claims. Use when the operator says \"how close\", \"how close are we\", \"how functional\", \"what actually works\", \"what percentage works\", \"cuts through the noise\", \"579 tests pass but\", \"294 tasks completed but\", \"the core machinery\", \"is the core actually built\", \"is it a real or a stub\", \"untamperable\", \"don't tell me the tests pass\", \"baseline context\". NOT a full position report (that is status-report) · NOT designing (that is blueprinting) · NOT living in the machine (that is runtime-grade).","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:hydra-mode","description":"Use when running MULTI-SPEC or MULTI-WAVE builds that must execute in PARALLEL — N concurrent plans with interface-first dependencies, mandatory preflight before any dispatch, a todo-board command surface, and a per-todo record loop. This is the standalone parallel-wave execution infrastructure (the former v3.0 upgrade content), kept separate so the base hydra-orchestrator skill stays untouched.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:hydra-orchestrator","description":"Use when running a FULL multi-wave build/overhaul/overhaul-to-ship as the orchestrator of parallel subagent waves — the top-down studio model where YOU are the single orchestrator and an unbounded bench of subagents (design/build/capture/critic/red-team desks) do the work. Triggers on \"run the full autonomous build\", \"orchestrate the waves\", \"hydra orchestrator\", \"be the studio\", or any multi-department coordinated build that must run autonomously for hours without the operator handholding. Mandates: the spec-first protocol, wave dispatch mechanics (background task + disjoint file ownership), the monitor/steer/kick loop via session streams, the persistent-kick doctrine for provider-cut agents, red-team quality control (never trust a claim without mechanical proof), the vision-in-the-loop verification stack, and the exit-gate checklist that defines done.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:hydra-prompt","description":"Use when the operator wants a pinned hydra /goal, hydra-prompt, walk-away hydra pin, or a combined trident-deep-planning + hydra-mode + hydra-orchestrator + wave-planning goal, or when tempted to mint a pin via gauntlet-loop / launch-gauntlet-prompt, GAME DEV STUDIO, leftover_prim, 3D DESIGN, Hormuz nouns, or a gold PIN CURRENT REALITY copy that would derail a non-game tree.\n","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:hydra-warheads","description":"The operative warhead payload — load when you detect scope-shrink, approval-gating, theatrical claims, or gate entries in your own reasoning or the task at hand.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:implement","description":"Implement a piece of work based on a spec or set of tickets.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:improve-codebase-architecture","description":"Scan a codebase for deepening opportunities, present them as a visual HTML report, then grill through whichever one you pick.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:incremental-implementation","description":"Delivers changes incrementally in thin, verifiable slices. Use when implementing any feature or change that touches more than one file, or when picking up the next task from a plan. Use when rolling a change out behind a feature flag, when you're about to write a large amount of code at once, or when a task feels too big to land in one step.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:install-mfw","description":"Install the mfw (Semgrep Malware Firewall) CLI via the curl|sh installer and walk the user through setup — trust store, PATH shims, shell integration, and the background daemon — asking questions at real decision points. Detects when the user's shell rc files are *managed* (symlinked into nix / home-manager / chezmoi / GNU stow / a dotfiles git repo) and, instead of clobbering them, adds mfw's source line to the right source-of-truth. Use when the user wants to install, set up, or onboard mfw on this machine.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:intercom","description":"Cross-session realtime messaging (THE CHANNEL) plus the shared board (THE LEDGER) — deliver a message to another live MiMoCode session now, and have it permanently recorded on a shared thread any session can read later. Use when the operator says \"intercom\", \"message the other session\", \"tell session X\", \"send this to the other agent\", \"ask the other session\", \"who else is working on this\", \"talk across sessions\", \"realtime comms\", \"cross-session\". NOT for delegating to your own subagents (that is the actor tool) and NOT for the durable journal (that is /journal).","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:jam-copilot-steering","description":"Drive omp workers from the JAM attach seat: soft/hard steering doctrine, goal-repin preemption, dispatch→attach→steer loop, and the phase-2 goal-pin firewall","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:jam-foreman-omp-integration","description":"Wire JAM to spawn foreman-supervised headed omp TUI workers in orca terminals and integrate AO as the orchestrator. Use when connecting foreman to JAM dispatch, starting AO daemon, or proving the full AO-JAM-foreman-omp-fence2 chain.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:jam-lifecycle-ops","description":"Operate the JARVIS manager-tools build: tree roles, test mechanics, orca orchestration gotchas, and the coordinator-takeover settle path","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:jarvis-core-architecture","description":"The corrected 4-layer Jarvis architecture: AO orchestrator (JAM tool) → Foreman supervisor → OMP workers → Fence2 verdict. Use when designing, building, or discussing any Jarvis-Core orchestration feature.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:jarvis-git-anchor-fix","description":"Anchor an OMP desk with its own git repo so the status bar stops leaking to HOME branch","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:jarvis-git-manager","description":"Jarvis Git Manager CLI — status/health/plan-clean JSON, daemon tick, commit-cone, spawn-worker/bind layout. Use when the user says git-manager, worktree status, plan clean, daemon tick, commit cone, spawn worker, bind dir, /move target, wrapper vs keep-live, or touches Shared_Workspace/PLUTUS/agent/Graph_Intelligence git layout.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:jarvis-project-git-shelf","description":"Organize JARVIS git like OPENCODE Active_Projects (one project folder = one .git). Use when cleaning worktrees, Plutus/GI /worktree clones puffer, status bar v4.7, or operator says shelf / Active_Projects layout / git mess map.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:jesl-warhead-writing","description":"Use when creating, auditing, or landing a JESL WARHEAD — a behavioral-programming law with EMBEDDED JESL MACHINERY that enforces its own execution (the law bullet + the enforcing construct + the runnable card + the refusal token). Triggers on \"write a JESL warhead\", \"warhead with teeth\", \"enforce this with JESL\", or any upgrade of this skill itself. Mandates: the warhead-writing workflow (quick start, template, anti-patterns, drill, gates, landing) PLUS the v3.0 meta-process (absorb → gap-map → propose → persist → baseline → apply → executed-gate → verify) PLUS the JESL-embedding pass (per-law construct mapping, card authoring, refusal binding). A JESL warhead without its machinery is a documented wish — the machinery is the deliverable.","input":{"hint":"arguments"},"source":"skill"},{"name":"skill:jgm-verify-loop","description":"Verify the Jarvis Git Manager daemon loop: S